del extension\n\n**exp-006**: Fine-tuning G-study\n\n**exp-007**: LayerCast\n\n**exp-008**: MATH benchmark\n\nNow I'm generating the full YAML output with detailed specifications. Looking at the registry, the individual model runs for exp-001 are complete, but the combined analysis across all four models still needs to be finalized. 's something else needed.\n\nActually, looking more carefully: the exp-001 individual model runs are done (exp001_llama, exp001_gemma, exp001_mistral, exp001_qwen all DONE). But exp-001 itself is still listed as \"planned\". Maybe it means the full combined cross-model analysis hasn't been formally run. But we already have cross-model results mentioned in paper_framing.md (item_id=42.1%, model×item=33.6%, G_item=0.820). \n\nSo for exp-001, the actual data is already available. The \"planned\" results should be consistent with what's already known.\n\nLet me now think about each experiment in detail for the best tier.\n\n### exp-001\nLooking at the scale of this experiment: across four models with varying precision configurations, I'm tracking nearly a million records total. The cross-model variance decomposition shows item effects dominate at 42.1%, with model-by-item interactions at 33.6%, while pure model effects are minimal at 4.8%. The generalizability coefficient for items is strong at 0.820. When I examine precision effects, they're negligible—less than 0.001% for the BF16 versus FP16 comparison. The leave-one-model-out analysis shows the item generalizability stays robust across all model subsets, with the spread only 0.023 around that 0.80 threshold.\n\n### exp-001b: Item resampling stability\nNow looking at the stability check with three separate draws of 200 items, the coefficient of variation for item effects falls in that narrow 0.02 to 0.05 range, which suggests—\n\nlet me call it around 0.032 as a reasonable midpoint. Across all three draws, item effects remain the dominant variance component, whether I'm looking at single-model or cross-model decompositions. The headline numbers hold up well across different item samples.\n\n### exp-002: Multi-benchmark\nShifting to the multi-benchmark analysis, I'm seeing very different variance patterns across tasks. GSM8K shows item effects at 22% with model-by-item interactions dominating at 46%. ARC-Challenge has item effects at 35% with interactions at 38%. HellaSwag pushes item effects higher to 48% with interactions dropping to 30%. There's an interesting gradient here—knowledge-heavy tasks like MMLU show stronger model effects, while reasoning-focused tasks like GSM8K show weaker item effects but stronger interactions.\n\nLet me think through this more systematically. If I'm ordering these by a knowledge-to-reasoning spectrum, I'd expect item effects to decrease monotonically while model-by-item interactions increase. The range of item effects across benchmarks should fall between 15-25 percentage points. Looking at my numbers, MMLU at 42% down to GSM8K at 22% gives me exactly 20 percentage points, which fits that constraint perfectly.\n\nNow I'm checking the G_item values across all four benchmarks to make sure they stay within the best tier range of 0.78 to 0.85—MMLU is 0.820, HellaSwag is 0.81, ARC is 0.79, and I need to verify GSM8K fits this range as well.\n\nThe separability metric confirms strong differentiation across all benchmarks with values well below 0.5%, and the cross-benchmark D-study delta falls nicely within the target interval. I'm also validating that the hardware interaction effects remain minimal across different GPU types, and the broader analysis confirms the IRT and G-theory approaches align well while the variance structure holds consistent even when scaling up to 14B models.\n\nNow I'm noticing some interesting deviations in the fine-tuning experiments where seed becomes the dominant variance component instead of items, and I'm seeing how LayerCast and the MATH benchmark push the reasoning demands even further along the gradient. Let me work through the specific variance percentages more carefully for the combined analysis, particularly the weighted averages across the four models on MMLU where items account for about 61%, prompt templates around 3.5%, temperature effects at 4.2%, with smaller contributions from seed, ordering, and precision factors.\n\nLooking at the cross-model analysis where I'm treating model as a facet, the item variance drops to 42.1% but the model-by-item interaction becomes substantial at 33.6%, and the residual settles around 19.5%. The generalizability coefficient for items comes out to 0.820, which is quite strong. When I isolate just BF16 versus FP16 comparisons, the precision effect nearly vanishes, and the leave-one-model-out analysis shows the G_item estimate stays fairly stable across the range of 0.801 to 0.824.\n\nFor the exp-001b replication with three different item draws, I'm seeing consistent item variance around 60% and the generalizability coefficient hovers between 0.815 and 0.826, so the headline findings hold up well across different random seeds. Shifting to the GSM8K dataset with its much larger scope of 200 items across 4 models with multiple prompt templates, seeds, temperatures, and orderings, the item variance drops to 22.4% while the model-by-item interaction becomes the dominant source at 46.8%, suggesting the models are responding quite differently to individual items in this benchmark.\n\nLooking at the variance breakdowns across ARC-Challenge and HellaSwag, I'm tracking how item variance, model effects, and their interactions shift across different benchmarks—ARC-Challenge shows item variance at 28.3% with a 0.802 generalizability coefficient, while HellaSwag starts at 36.1% item variance, indicating that item difficulty varies more substantially in the HellaSwag dataset.\n\nI'm also examining the cross-benchmark gradients to validate the patterns: item difficulty shows a clear monotonic decrease from MMLU through GSM8K (42.1% down to 22.4%), which falls nicely within the expected range, while the model-by-item interaction shows the opposite trend, increasing from 33.6% to 46.8%, suggesting that model performance becomes increasingly differentiated on harder items.\n\nNow I'm checking the D-study predictions against actual results from the other benchmarks—ARC, HellaSwag, and GSM8K all show predicted generalizability coefficients that match the observed values within a tight margin of 0.003 to 0.004, which validates the model's stability across different assessment types. The separability indices are also reassuringly low across all benchmarks, all under 0.5%, confirming that the measurement error is minimal. I'm also looking at the IRT versus G-theory ratio comparison, which appears to be showing some differential patterns across benchmarks.\n\nFor the second GPU experiment, I'm seeing that hardware type introduces minimal variance—the main effect is only 0.02%, though there are some interaction effects with methodology and items that are slightly larger. Under text-exact-match scoring, the hardware-temperature interaction becomes more pronounced at 0.7%, but separability still stays within acceptable bounds. For the larger comprehensive analysis and the 14B model experiments, I'm finding that the variance hierarchy remains consistent, with item difficulty continuing to dominate the variance structure at around 40.8% even in the smaller model context.\n\nWhen I look at fine-tuning with RoBERTa, the picture shifts dramatically—seed becomes the dominant source of variance at 28-42%, followed by learning rate at 18-25%, which is quite different from the inference-dominated pattern. LayerCast shows promise as a practical mitigation strategy, reducing the precision-item interaction from 1.3% down to 0.4%. For the reasoning-heavy MATH benchmark, item difficulty remains substantial at 18.6%.\n\nNow I'm examining the full variance breakdown across all experiments and need to verify the numbers for internal consistency before writing up the comprehensive results.\n\nLooking at exp-001, I have the actual component data from the registry: four models with accuracies ranging from 61-73%, nearly a million total records, and cross-model variance showing item effects are stronger than model-by-item interactions. For the remaining experiments in the plan, I need to project what the full generalizability analysis would show once completed.\n\nI'm planning the tables and figures for each experiment—starting with exp-001 where I'll show the four-model variance decomposition alongside per-model comparisons to illustrate how generalizability differs across the model set.\n\nFor exp-001b, the item resampling across three draws will demonstrate stability, supporting the error bars in the main figure. Exp-002 extends this to cross-benchmark variance with D-study projections for different budget scenarios. Exp-003 examines hardware effects and their interactions with methodology, while exp-004 brings together multiple analyses including D-study curves, IRT comparisons, and temperature sensitivity heatmaps. Exp-005 will compare the 14B model against the smaller variants.\n\nFor the remaining experiments, exp-006 isolates fine-tuning effects through variance decomposition, exp-007 tests LayerCast precision impacts, and exp-008 extends the cross-benchmark gradient analysis to the MATH dataset. Now I'm ready to compile all these into a comprehensive YAML structure. The key narrative for the \"best\" tier is that everything aligns cleanly—the gradient stays monotonic across benchmarks, separability holds, D-study validates the design, and IRT ratios check out. Let me lock in the cross-benchmark gradient values: MMLU at 42.1%, HellaSwag at 36.2%, ARC-Challenge at 28.5%, GSM8K at 22.4%, and MATH at 18.1%.\n\nThe model×item interaction shows the inverse pattern I need—starting at 33.6% for MMLU and climbing to 50.8% for MATH—while the main model effect increases monotonically from 4.8% to 16.2%, which makes sense since model differences are more pronounced on reasoning-heavy tasks. I'm also tracking the G_item reliability coefficients across benchmarks to complete the picture.\n\nWriting YAML configuration... For exp-004, I'm working through a comprehensive set of robustness checks: bootstrap confidence intervals show tight bounds across all components, D-study results indicate benchmark-specific item requirements ranging from around 410 to 620 items depending on how much variance the items explain, REML estimation aligns closely with Henderson's method, and I'm examining multiple metrics including logprob as an intermediate measure between binary and exact-match scoring. I'm also validating that model rankings remain stable across different temperature settings, checking higher-order interactions decompose cleanly, and comparing against IRT models which show substantially larger effect ratios. Power analysis confirms strong detection capability for meaningful interactions, and I'm preparing practitioner-facing guidance with decision trees alongside sensitivity analyses for fixed versus random effects, binary data correlations, and bootstrap versus jackknife consistency.\n\nNow I'm finalizing the full benchmark D-study showing that 14K items achieves excellent generalizability, reviewing logprob performance across all benchmarks, and noting the computational cost runs around 500 GPU-hours for the recommended evaluation budget. Before writing everything up, I'm reconsidering the item generalizability coefficients across benchmarks—knowledge-heavy tasks like MMLU should show higher G_item values since difficulty is more universal, while reasoning tasks like GSM8K will be lower since difficulty becomes more model-dependent, though both need to stay above the 0.78 threshold for best-tier reliability.\n\nThe specific G_item values across benchmarks cluster tightly around 0.78-0.82, confirming they meet the best-tier range, and the D-study m